# G099 — Code Snippet Manager

*v2. See `snippet-manager.md` for prose: tab-stop IR (text + stops in
lockstep), `$0` always last, three placeholder forms
(`${N:default}` / `${N}` / `$N`), duplicate stops preserved for
mirroring, library indexed by trigger / language / tag.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Code Snippets

[vault-code-snippets @snippet-dir
    IN ORDER TO
        load a library of JSON snippets from the snippets directory
        expand them on trigger match and hand text + stops to the UI
        so the user can tab through each placeholder in canonical order

    @lib   <- [snippet[library]]
    @files <- [fs[ls {path: @snippet-dir + "/*.json"}]]

    for @file in @files
        @data <- [json[parse {text: [fs[read-string {path: @file:path}]]}]]
        [snippet[library-add {library: @lib, snippet: @data}]]

    sync @user-events {
        trigger @trigger @lang ->
            @s <- [snippet[find-by-trigger
                             {library: @lib,
                              trigger: @trigger, language: @lang}]]
            @s
                where exists
                    @e <- [snippet[expand {snippet: @s}]]
                    [ui[insert-with-stops
                          {text: @e:text, stops: @e:stops}]]
                otherwise
                    -> [ui[toast {msg: "no snippet for " + @trigger}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[snippet
    library
    library-add            {library, snippet}
    find-by-trigger        {library, trigger, language}   ;; -> Snippet | none
    find-by-language       {library, language}
    find-by-tag            {library, tag}
    expand                 {snippet}                       ;; -> Expansion
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: placeholder parsing as three `where` forms

[snippet[expand {snippet: @s}]
    IN ORDER TO
        walk @s:body character-by-character producing text and stops
        so the editor receives both pieces of the snippet contract

    for @ch in @s:body
        where @ch is "$" followed by "{N:default}"
            -> emit default into text, record stop (N, offset, length)
        where @ch is "$" followed by "{N}"
            -> record stop (N, offset, 0)
        where @ch is "$" followed by digits
            -> record stop (parsed N, offset, 0)
        otherwise
            -> copy @ch verbatim into text

    stops ordered by
        @stop:number is zero sorts last (final cursor rule),
        @stop:number ascending otherwise
]

`$0` as the final resting place is a 20-year-old snippet-system
convention every editor ancestry (TextMate, VSCode, Sublime) shares.
Violating the rule breaks user expectations that they carry with them.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: duplicate stops preserved `where` mirroring is UI's job

[snippet[expand]
    emit both occurrences of ${1:name} as two stops with number 1
    IN ORDER TO
        let the editor group them by number and mirror edits
        while keeping the parser's output purely structural
]

The library does not deduplicate. The consumer does. Mirroring is a
UI concern; the parser provides raw grouping data.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @lib <- [snippet[library]]
    [snippet[library-add
              {library: @lib,
               snippet: {trigger: "for", language: "python",
                         tags: ["loop"],
                         body: "for ${1:item} in ${2:collection}:\n" +
                                "    ${3:pass}\n$0"}}]]

    @s <- [snippet[find-by-trigger
                     {library: @lib,
                      trigger: "for", language: "python"}]]

    -> [snippet[expand {snippet: @s}]]
    ;; text: "for item in collection:\n    pass\n"
    ;; stops: [#1 @4/4, #2 @12/10, #3 @28/4, #0 @33/0]
    ;;        (non-zero ascending, then 0 last)
]
